From: khj809 <onsealeatang@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 18:14:33 +0900
Subject: tsi: a socket listened on again stays where its first listen put it

## Why virtx needs it

A program that listens on a socket twice -- nginx does, to set its backlog -- had its second listen sent to the policy, which, the port being held by the first, put it elsewhere; the host's socket stayed where it was, and the guest's port was reached at neither from then on.

## When it can go

With the TsiPolicy patch, which it corrects.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/devices/src/virtio/vsock/muxer.rs | 13 ++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/devices/src/virtio/vsock/muxer.rs b/src/devices/src/virtio/vsock/muxer.rs
index b4e258e9..1b3a3ec5 100644
--- a/src/devices/src/virtio/vsock/muxer.rs
+++ b/src/devices/src/virtio/vsock/muxer.rs
@@ -587,7 +587,18 @@ impl VsockMuxer {
                 return;
             }
             debug!("DGRAM listen request: id={id}");
-            if let Some(at) = inet(&req.addr)
+            // A socket listening already, listened on again -- as nginx does, to set its backlog
+            // -- stays where it is: the host's socket was bound where the policy said at its
+            // first `listen`, and is not bound again. Asked again, the policy would say where
+            // it is now (another port, the first being held), and be wrong from then on.
+            let listening = self.proxy_map.read().unwrap().get(&id).is_some_and(|proxy| {
+                matches!(
+                    proxy.lock().unwrap().status(),
+                    ProxyStatus::Listening | ProxyStatus::WaitingOnAccept
+                )
+            });
+            if !listening
+                && let Some(at) = inet(&req.addr)
                 && let Some(policy) = &self.policy
             {
                 match policy.listen(at) {
